Let me take one more stab at this.

There seem to be two core reasons why people dislike pudding farming:
     1. Farming is a profitable activity that can be engaged in with
minimal thought and even more minimal effort (on most platforms) by
repeating a short command sequence 60,000 times.
     2. Farming leads to unlimited items.

I agree wholeheartedly that reason one is a bad thing and should be
changed. I disagree vehemently that reason two is a bad thing.

If someone decides that there should not be more than a "reasonable"
quantity of items available, the next question of course is what is a
"reasonable" quantity?

If The Powers That Be should decide that "having huge piles of loot"
is itself undesirable, then naturally we expect them to change other
mechanisms that lead to "excess" items. All forms of farming are
certainly out. Maybe altar camping should be abolished as well.
Perhaps smoky potions should no longer provide wishes. Perhaps there
should be no randomly generated wands of wishing. Perhaps death drops
should be made rarer. Perhaps there should be no death drops at all.

This is not primarily a "slippery slope" argument, it's an argument
about the desirable and undesirable qualities of the game. While I
admire and commend those who can ascend on a shoestring, I don't
necessarily want to play that way myself. I feel strongly that the
*possibility* of being able to acquire "huge piles of loot" is a
desirable quality of the game and should be preserved. I just think it
should be made less automatable.

